WEBVTT

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.400
Speaker 1: So today we're talking about redemption.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.920
Speaker 1: And I want to start by talking about a very beautiful law that was embedded in Jewish practice, all right?

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.840
Speaker 1: The concept of a king's man redeemer.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.440
Speaker 1: So some of you might have heard the phrase before.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.400
Speaker 1: Maybe you don't really particularly know what it's about.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:43.840
Speaker 1: So a kinsman redeemer was actually a relative who had the responsibility of rescuing or redeeming somebody who found himself in a situation of loss or bondage.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:50.760
Speaker 1: And it was typically, all right, kicked into effect if four things happened, all right?

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.380
Speaker 1: The first is if there was a need to redeem land.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:18.980
Speaker 1: So if a relative sold his inheritance due to poverty, like he became so poor that he sold his inheritance, God already factored in, all right, into the Jewish law, a system of grace where a relative can come and redeem back that property, pay money for it, buy it back on your behalf.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:26.180
Speaker 1: You know, in this dog eats dog society, well, I mean, it's all man for himself.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:41.140
Speaker 1: But just imagine that this, that even under the law there was a system of grace where if you fell short financially, somebody, all right, who is your relative, can come and, you know, salvage the situation, paying redemption for you.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.590
Speaker 1: Just for extra references, you can check Leviticus 25:25, all right?

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:53.270
Speaker 1: It was also kicked into effect if, like in those days, you became so poor that you sold yourself into slavery.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:02:02.310
Speaker 1: So if you've heard me teach on slavery, you have to understand that slavery in those days was actually like an economic plan for most people.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:10.830
Speaker 1: Of course, there was still some slavery, like getting slaves as dividends from battles and wars.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:19.260
Speaker 1: But some people volunteer, voluntarily sold themselves into slavery when they were poor, all right?

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.700
Speaker 1: And there were strict measures for their protection.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.140
Speaker 1: And all of that conversation for another day.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:38.820
Speaker 1: But at the end of the day, God still created this system as a panacea for people maybe who found themselves in financial trouble and were sold into slavery.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:46.760
Speaker 1: It was just a system of grace so that you could redeem your relatives because God didn't want any of his people to be slaves.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:47.480
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:58.680
Speaker 1: And then the third, and those of you who are familiar with the Ruth Naomi story will know this one, is to provide an heir through levirate marriage.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.000
Speaker 1: And what's that?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:14.760
Speaker 1: It's just an old Jewish law where if anybody, any man died and he had no heir instead of his lineage just being extinguished like that.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.920
Speaker 1: Listen, and don't use your 21st century lens to look at this, all right?

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.720
Speaker 1: Any of his brothers could marry his wife to, in a sense, continue his legacy.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.440
Speaker 1: So now look at it this way.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.630
Speaker 1: As in this case of Ruth and, you know, Naomi's suggestions.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:39.670
Speaker 1: It was something that the women most, most times wanted in that culture.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.390
Speaker 1: It wasn't something forceful.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:42.070
Speaker 1: It was something.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:47.350
Speaker 1: It was actually a law that was seen as something graceful.

00:03:47.509 --> 00:03:48.070
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.750
Speaker 1: You know, in that day and age.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:50.870
Speaker 1: All right?

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.430
Speaker 1: And then also it was done to avenge blood.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:59.670
Speaker 1: Like, if somebody kills a relative, then your kinsman redeemer goes and fights for you.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:09.950
Speaker 1: Pay attention to the fact that the kinsman redeemer would also often pay ransom to free any of his relatives that were sold into slavery.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.270
Speaker 1: I'm going to come back to that.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:12.390
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:26.230
Speaker 1: Now, from a theological standpoint, understand that this was actually a very powerful spiritual metaphor of Christ and what Christ was going to come and do.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:32.690
Speaker 1: All right, in Hebrews, chapter two, from verse 14, he became a human, meaning he became kinsman.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:33.730
Speaker 1: All right?

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.090
Speaker 1: So it's very fitting that we can call him a kinsman redeemer.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.450
Speaker 1: And, of course, he paid ransom with his blood.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.650
Speaker 1: First Peter, chapter one, verse 18 to 19.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:49.170
Speaker 1: Okay, so as we come to redemption, now, how should you understand this?

00:04:49.570 --> 00:05:00.670
Speaker 1: Redemption is simply the gracious act of God that describes Christ paying for your sins with his blood so that you can be free.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:05.350
Speaker 1: And I'm just paraphrasing, all right, let me put it this way.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.390
Speaker 1: What does it mean to redeem?

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:08.430
Speaker 1: All right.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:13.790
Speaker 1: It simply means to free somebody who is in bondage by paying ransom.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.190
Speaker 1: I take that again.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.070
Speaker 1: What does it mean to be to redeem?

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:21.030
Speaker 1: It simply means to free somebody who is in bondage by paying ransom.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:34.870
Speaker 1: And so in the context of salvation, all right, it describes the gracious act of God in giving Jesus as ransom for your sins so that you can be free from bondage.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:36.870
Speaker 1: It's very powerful.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:50.630
Speaker 1: And, you know, I was reading ancient material talking about the ancient practice of ransom, and the writer was expecting that we will not be able to relate in 2025.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:06:01.240
Speaker 1: But in fact, for some of you listening to me, maybe ransom is a trigger word for you because you have some distant relative or somebody that knows somebody that was actually kidnapped.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:01.520
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:02.640
Speaker 1: In 2025.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.080
Speaker 1: And you had to pay ransom for the person to be freed.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:06.800
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.560
Speaker 1: So the keywords redeem and ransom actually go hand in hand.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.360
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.600
Speaker 1: So redeem is to free somebody from bondage by paying ransom.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.830
Speaker 1: So they actually, indeed go hand in hand.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:29.270
Speaker 1: And as we begin to unpack this, understand that this is what Christ did for us.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:38.190
Speaker 1: The Bible says in Ephesians 1:7, it says in him you have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:39.150
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:45.670
Speaker 1: And I was studying the word forgiveness and what it actually means in the Greek.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:48.690
Speaker 1: And the Greek word that was used actually means to release.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:49.730
Speaker 1: To release.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:59.250
Speaker 1: And it was old expression to describe somebody who was kept in bondage and was released.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:08.210
Speaker 1: So it's just telling you the forgiveness of Christ was his ransom, freeing you from the bondage of sin.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:14.490
Speaker 1: And that's why Paul puts it that way in Ephesians 1:7, in whom we have redemption through his blood.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.850
Speaker 1: We have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.690
Speaker 1: So redemption and forgiveness go hand in hand.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.730
Speaker 1: So your forgiveness came at a cost.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:25.570
Speaker 1: At the cost.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.850
Speaker 1: God didn't just say, I forgive you.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:30.290
Speaker 1: All right?

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.130
Speaker 1: It came at a cost.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.650
Speaker 1: We have redemption through his blood.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:39.970
Speaker 1: His blood was the ransom.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:42.090
Speaker 1: His blood was the ransom.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:50.170
Speaker 1: And, you know, now this opens a very long theological debate about who the ransom was paid to.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.530
Speaker 1: And you see, there are many views on this.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.410
Speaker 1: You've heard of pennile substitution.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:56.850
Speaker 1: You've heard of.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:57.250
Speaker 1: Some of.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.769
Speaker 1: You might have heard of Christus Victor.

00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:01.250
Speaker 1: If you've not heard of that, don't worry, all right?

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:07.010
Speaker 1: But I think for me, the most satisfactory understanding of ransom, okay?

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:12.300
Speaker 1: Especially in 2025, where you see ransom as paying money to somebody.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:14.700
Speaker 1: But think about it this way.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:19.020
Speaker 1: Remember the movie Titanic?

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:27.180
Speaker 1: There was a floating wood, and only one person could stay on that wood.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:28.940
Speaker 1: And don't get me started.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.700
Speaker 1: I think that movie was really annoying because it was supposed to be romantic.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.659
Speaker 1: But I'm just wondering, why didn't they take turns?

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:40.669
Speaker 1: All right, if you are cold, you stay in the water for 10 minutes, then we switch.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:43.590
Speaker 1: But Mr.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.950
Speaker 1: Romantic died, and he's not Jesus Christ anyway, so.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.310
Speaker 1: But the idea is this.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:09:04.860
Speaker 1: If two people could not stay and stay afloat at the same time, and so the guy stayed in the water and died in the water, you could say that he gave his life for ransom, as a ransom, to save a life.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:11.420
Speaker 1: Now, when you're thinking about that, you don't say, to whom did he pay the ransom?

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.580
Speaker 1: To who did he pay the ransom?

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:14.620
Speaker 1: That's not.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:19.900
Speaker 1: You're looking at it from just one usage of the word ransom.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:26.700
Speaker 1: If someone is living at your expense, that's still ransom, whether or not you pay it to anybody.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.740
Speaker 1: God came into the world, you know, to die for us.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:39.020
Speaker 1: So I quoted Ephesians, chapter 1, verse 7, in whom you have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:44.060
Speaker 1: Colossians 1:14 actually says almost exactly the same thing.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:49.180
Speaker 1: It says, in whom we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:53.540
Speaker 1: I mean, the only difference, I think Ephesians 1:7 says, according to riches of his grace.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:10:00.580
Speaker 1: Okay, Then he now says in verse 15, he is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creature.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:07.220
Speaker 1: For by him all things were created that are in heaven and that were on earth, visible and invisible.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:13.300
Speaker 1: Whether thrones or dominion, principalities or powers, all things were created through him and for Him.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:23.790
Speaker 1: And so it's a very powerful picture that this kinsman, this Redeemer, was in fact the creator of the whole world.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.830
Speaker 1: He took our place, died our death.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.110
Speaker 1: Hallelujah.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:29.070
Speaker 1: All right.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:31.110
Speaker 1: And so when you recognize this.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.070
Speaker 1: Oh, my God, you think about what Paul said to the Church at Rome.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.830
Speaker 1: When he says, who is it that condemns?

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.030
Speaker 1: He says, it's Christ.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.270
Speaker 1: Christ who even died for us.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:46.190
Speaker 1: So the judge is the one who paid the price.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.010
Speaker 1: He knows the requirements, and he paid it in full.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:55.170
Speaker 1: And because of what he has done, you are free from any guilt of sin.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.690
Speaker 1: If you are in Christ, listen to me.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:59.930
Speaker 1: Your ransom has been paid and paid in full.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:05.090
Speaker 1: So like we said yesterday, there is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:07.690
Speaker 1: No condemnation, no condemnation.

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:09.610
Speaker 1: No, listen to me.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:15.780
Speaker 1: You see, your past might still be in your memory.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.700
Speaker 1: You still think of all the things you did when you were in the world, all the mistakes you made.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:26.340
Speaker 1: You know, every one of us has these moments where we just have flashbacks.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.500
Speaker 1: I remember some silly things that we did.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:34.820
Speaker 1: And you still feel the present guilt, you know, the present shame because of what you've done.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:36.180
Speaker 1: But listen to me.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:42.990
Speaker 1: In him you have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.230
Speaker 1: The guilt of your sin has been washed away forever.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:51.470
Speaker 1: If you are in Christ, so walk in the newness of life.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:52.990
Speaker 1: Hallelujah.

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:56.350
Speaker 1: And I don't want you to just see this as a theological discourse.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:12:02.390
Speaker 1: I want you to see it as the fact, the spiritual reality that it is.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.390
Speaker 1: If you are in Christ Jesus, he has made you free.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:09.420
Speaker 1: And you must walk in that liberty and with thanksgiving, okay?

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:15.420
Speaker 1: For who the Son of Man has set free is free indeed.
